                                            <td class="resolutiondate"> &nbsp; </td>
                                            <td class="versions">                        1.7.6            </td>
                                            <td class="fixVersions">    &nbsp;
</td>
                                            <td class="components">                        c++            </td>
                                            <td class="duedate">    &nbsp;
</td>
                                            <td class="votes">0</td>
                                            <td class="watches">1</td>
                                            <td class="thumbnail"></td>
                                            <td class="timeoriginalestimate"></td>
                                            <td class="timeestimate"></td>
                                            <td class="timespent"></td>
                                            <td class="workratio">&nbsp;
</td>
                                            <td class="subtasks">                </td>
                                            <td class="issuelinks">                </td>
                                            <td class="environment">      Win32
  </td>
                                            <td class="description">      FileBufferCopyIn::seek() on a Windows system generally fails with an exception. This is because it is implemented as<br/>
<br/>
if (::SetFilePointer(...)  != INVALID_SET_FILE_POINTER) throw(...)<br/>
<br/>
This test is the opposite of what it should be! SetFilePointer returns the new pointer on success (sort of), just like lseek. See http://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/library/windows/desktop/aa365541%28v=vs.85%29.aspx<br/>
<br/>
The fix is pretty simple - reverse the test. But apparently INVALID_SET_FILE_POINTER is also a valid file pointer (that&#39;s not confusing at all - thanks Microsoft!) so you also need to check the Windows error indicator to make absolutely sure. I&#39;ll attach a patch.
  </td>
